 "etapas": [
  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "〜そうです: looks like (appearance)",
     "texto": [
      "With -i adjectives, drop the **い** and add **そう です**: **おいしい** → **おいしそう です** (looks tasty).",
      "With verbs, use the -masu stem without ます + **そう です**: **ふります** → **ふりそう です** (looks like it's going to rain)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "〜そうです with -na adjectives",
     "texto": [
      "With a -na adjective, **そうです** is added without **な**: **げんき** → **げんきそうです** (looks well). With **いい** the form is **よさそうです** (looks good)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "〜そうです: apparently",
     "texto": [
      "With the plain form + **そうです** you report something you have heard: **かえる そうです** (apparently they're returning). With nouns and -na adjectives **だ** is kept: **あした は やすみ だそうです** (apparently tomorrow is a holiday).",
      "Don't confuse this use with **おいしそうです** (looks tasty), which expresses a visual impression."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "〜らしい: apparently; so I hear",
     "texto": [
      "**〜らしい** conveys an inference or indirect information: **あした は やすみ らしい です** (apparently tomorrow is a holiday). It isn't simply «more general» than **〜そうです**: the nuance depends on whether a visible impression or something heard is being presented."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
